{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And he expired and was gathered to his people.</b> And it did not mention death regarding him, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'Our father Jacob did not die.'" "<b>And behold,</b> this parashah testifies that he died, and that the Egyptians embalmed him and mourned him for seventy days, and afterward his sons buried him in the Cave of Machpelah, the burial place of his fathers." "<b>But</b> the meaning of 'did not die' is that he did not taste the taste of death; or the sense of 'did not die' is that the soul of Jacob hovered constantly over his body by virtue of the power of his holiness, for the souls of other righteous ones who are not at his level of holiness return to their root and their source, and once they have ascended they do not descend; but Jacob, by virtue of the holiness of his body and his exalted rank, his soul would ascend and descend, and this great power is found only among the holy ones who are in the earth, the unique ones of the generations, such as our holy Rabbi. And from this our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in Ketubot, chapter HaNose (Ketubot 103a): 'Every erev Shabbat he would come to his house. One erev Shabbat he came and knocked at the door. His maidservant said: Be silent, for the Rabbi is sitting.' When Rabbi heard this, he did not come again, so as not to cast aspersion upon the earlier righteous ones — meaning that their souls had returned to their root and they had not attained this exalted level, to have such great power as our holy Rabbi; all the more so our father Jacob, who was sanctified with three sanctifications: with the holiness of Abraham, who was sanctified by the commandment of circumcision before he begot Isaac; with the holiness of Isaac, who was sanctified by the Akedah upon the altar to be a perfect burnt offering; and with the holiness of himself, for he is the third of the fathers, the completion of the Chariot, and there was no blemish in his seed. And for this reason it appears proper to say that the Holy One, blessed be He, is called by his name (Isaiah 29:23): 'קדוש יעקב' — 'the Holy One of Jacob' — and we do not find 'קדוש אברהם' or 'קדוש יצחק' (the Holy One of Abraham, the Holy One of Isaac)." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> 'our father Jacob did not die' — for his attribute was not like the matter of the other extremal attributes, which produce opposites. And the explanation of this is that his attribute is the decisive and balancing one; and even though it is comprised of all the attributes and receives from them, since it ascends by a paved pathway up to the Beginning of Beginning, which is the source of life, therefore they said of Jacob 'he did not die,' and they did not say thus of Abraham and Isaac, because they are extremal attributes, to the right and to the left, which produce opposites. Therefore our father Jacob did not die, but remained enduring in body and in soul. This body is the second, refined body in which the soul is clothed in the form of a body, and it has substance, but it is extremely refined; it is clothed in it at appointed times — from erev Shabbat to erev Shabbat, or from Yom Kippur to Yom Kippur — and it goes about in the world by the commission of the Holy One, blessed be He, and appears to whomever he wishes. For the first, coarse body was embalmed and buried according to the plain sense of Scripture. And this is the statement of the Sage who said: 'I interpret a verse, as it is said (Jeremiah 46:27): \"הנני מושיעך ואת זרעך\" — \"Behold, I will save you and your seed\"; he juxtaposes him to his seed: just as his seed is alive, so too is he alive' — meaning, just as his seed is alive in body and in soul, so too is he alive in body and in soul, and that is the body I have mentioned." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that it was to this that they alluded in Eikhah Rabbah, where they interpreted there (Lamentations 2:3): 'ויבער ביעקב כאש להבה' — 'and He burned in Jacob like a flaming fire' — when punishment comes upon Israel, none senses it but Jacob, as it is said: 'ויבער ביעקב'; and when goodness comes upon Israel, none senses it but Jacob, as it is said (Psalms 14:7): 'יגל יעקב ישמח ישראל' — end of quotation. And it is well known that the expression 'senses' does not apply to a body that was embalmed and buried, but only to the refined body that has substance and is possessed of sensation. And this is the matter of our holy Rabbi in Ketubot, and of Kapodeya in tractate Shabbat, chapter HaSho'el; all the more so in the case of Jacob, whose likeness is engraved upon the Throne of Glory. And this is the secret of the verse (Proverbs 10:2): 'וצדקה תציל ממות' — 'and righteousness delivers from death'; and this is 'זוכר יצוריו לחיים ברחמים' — 'who remembers His creatures for life with compassion' — for the existence of life is from compassion, and this is what David, peace be upon him, called (Psalms 84:5): 'יעקב סלה' — understand this. And from this the receivers of truth said in the Midrash (Genesis 28:12): 'עולים ויורדים בו' — ascending and descending upon him — they ascend above and see the likeness of Jacob engraved upon the Throne of Glory; they descend below and find him sleeping.'"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:00.991054533Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}